INV-lupav L2 -- the section-3a walk returns False (accounted-for) for a
PARTIALLY recorded definition, not just a barren one -- reproduces live at
dev tip on the item's faithful defs_at-seeded repro, with all controls
passing (recorded use -> True, barren -> None, gate ON -> None).

The remedy exists and is deliberately unwired on that arm: section 3a spells
its call '_ddg_taint_reaches(...) is True', so False and None collapse into
one 'adjudicated', whose only consequence is the finding's confidence and
analysis_method label. Wiring forfeit_refutation there today is observably
nothing and cannot carry a behavioural test. The hazard is the ORDERING --
the moment section 3a consumes a False the way the barrier arm already does
(PR #214, where a False earns 'sanitized' and DROPS the flow from a claim's
violation set), every unearned False deletes a real finding.

That instruction lived only in a tracker thread and a source comment, and a
decision that lives only in prose dies at the next compaction. It is now a
structural guard: either arm consuming the walk's False without passing
forfeit_refutation fails the build. The guard carries its own positive
controls, because a lint that cannot be shown to fire is indistinguishable
from one that matches nothing -- the failure mode of the retired
test_barren_seed_line_is_skipped, which passed for an unrelated reason while
guarding the exact line that was wrong.

Measurement recorded on INV-lupav and INV-busis (no code change): across 85
default-catalogue production walks on two repos and two languages the walk
returned False ZERO times, and the barrier arm -- which both items record as
having 'run 0 times on every measured repo' -- runs 21 times on alertmanager
once a sanitizer exists for the flow's label. Barrier-arm reachability is a
property of the sanitizer catalogue, not of the code.

